// A 2-component dot product is DP2, not DP3 (t_a30159bf).
//
// The general path chose DP4 for operand width >= 4 and DP3 for everything
// else, so `dot(v, v)` on a float2 read a THIRD lane that nothing wrote:
// the multiply before it carries a two-lane write mask, and whatever the
// allocator left in z went into the sum.  Not reliably wrong, which is
// worse - the same source is right in one shader and wrong in the next,
// depending on register assignment.
//
// The reference emits DP2 here.  NV40 has the opcode; we simply had no
// VOp for it.  Width 1 is deliberately NOT part of this fixture: nobody
// has measured what the reference does with a scalar dot, and guessing it
// into the same change would be the same mistake in the other direction.
//
// Reachable through dot, length, distance, normalize and reflect on any
// float2 - test_57_discard in the corpus computes length(float2), which is
// why its pixels could not be judged until this landed.
void main(float4 c : TEXCOORD0, out float4 o : COLOR)
{
    float2 v = c.xy * 8.0;
    float d = dot(v, v);
    o = float4(d, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0);
}
